Give be-lua's runtime std/net, through a pluggable client (temper.set_net_client) whose default is LuaSocket's socket.http, on sockets that never block a turn.
Upstream: not yet; to offer Kotlin Temper (Kotlin's Lua calls temper.sendrequest, which its runtime lacks).

Requests run in coroutines of their own over non-blocking sockets, which
an I/O source (temper.add_io_source) steps with socket.select; the drain
waits on I/O sources only when no step is ready, as nothing else drives
them. Without LuaSocket (or under a Lua it isn't built for), a request
gives a broken promise, as std says a backend that can't send gives, and
says how to plug in a client. No HTTPS: that needs LuaSec.

